\chapter{Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR)}

\section{Overview of AMR}
Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) is used to dynamically increase resolution in
regions of high curvature, strong tidal gradients, or horizon formation. The
SIM-COSMIC-CODE engine implements a 7-level refinement tree.

\section{Refinement Criteria}
Refinement is triggered when:
\[
\left\Vert \nabla R \right\Vert > \epsilon_R,
\qquad
|H| > \epsilon_H,
\qquad
|M_i| > \epsilon_M,
\]
where $\epsilon_R$, $\epsilon_H$, and $\epsilon_M$ are threshold parameters.

\section{Tree Structure}
Each refinement level subdivides a cell into 8 child cells. The maximum depth is:
\[
L_{\max} = 7.
\]

\section{Horizon Tracking}
Marginally trapped surfaces (MOTS) are detected using:
\[
\theta_{(\ell)} = 0,
\]
where $\theta_{(\ell)}$ is the expansion of outgoing null geodesics.

\section{Constraint Monitoring}
Hamiltonian and momentum constraints are monitored:
\[
H = R + K^2 - K_{ij}K^{ij},
\qquad
M_i = \nabla_j (K^j_{\ i} - \delta^j_{\ i} K).
\]

